## 5. Auth strategies for subdomain (choose one)

| Strategy | How | Pros | Cons |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **A. Query token (simplest)** | `302` → `https://{subhost}/?coder_session_token=…` | Already verified; no cookie on wildcard | Token briefly in URL / Referer; use short-lived session keys |
| **B. Bootstrap on app host** | Tiny middle (or edge) endpoint on `*.coder-a…` sets host cookie then redirects clean | Clean URL like path flow | Needs wildcard TLS + something listening on app hosts (or Coder itself accepting Set-Cookie from a bounce) |
| **C. Parent Domain cookie** | `Set-Cookie; Domain=.coder-a.example.com` | Cookie shared by dashboard + `*.` apps | Broader blast radius; avoid on shared parent like `.example.com`; **do not** use one Domain across planes |
| **D. Middle app proxy** | Keep traffic on middle `/proxy/...` | One origin | Not “open real Coder URL”; extra hop (we already have path proxy for some cases) |

**Recommendation for first production cut:** **A** (query token 302 to subdomain URL), same as today’s cross-host path fallback. Add **B** or carefully scoped **C** later if product forbids tokens in the address bar.

Path apps can keep today’s host-only cookie 302 when middle and plane share a cookie host.

